---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ipsas/ipsas22.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  covers={metadata.covers}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Objective, Scope & Definitions" },
        { id: "s2", label: "The Three Statistical Sectors — GGS, PFC & PNFC" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Relationship to Segment Reporting" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Statistical Bases vs IPSAS Financial Statements" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Accounting Policies for GGS Disclosures" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Further Disaggregation" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Required Disclosures" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Reconciliations" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Statement Template — Illustrative GGS SOFP" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Effective Date & Amendment Timeline" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Relationship to Statistical Reporting Standards" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement / Process Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="active"
    explanation="IPSAS 22 Disclosure of Financial Information about the General Government Sector is the current, active standard issued by the IPSASB for presenting general government sector financial information, including statistical-sector classification, reconciliations, and an illustrative GGS statement of financial position. Effective 1 January 2008."
    historical="IPSAS 22 is a public-sector-specific Standard with no private-sector equivalent; it complements IPSAS 18 Segment Reporting by separating statistical-sector (GGS/PFC/PNFC) presentation of budgetary and financial information from entity-level segment disclosure."
    link=""
    linkText=""
  />

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 — OBJECTIVE, SCOPE & DEFINITIONS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Objective, Scope &amp; Definitions (paras. 1–4, 15)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Objective &amp; scope</div>
        <div class="principle">Prescribes disclosure requirements for governments that ELECT to present information about the General Government Sector (GGS) in their consolidated financial statements — enhancing transparency and the link between market/non-market activities and between IPSAS statements and statistical reporting bases.</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Applies to a government preparing consolidated accrual-basis financial statements that ELECTS to disclose GGS information</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush">This Standard is entirely OPTIONAL — disclosure is ALLOWED, never required. Only GOVERNMENTS (not individual controlled entities) can meaningfully present a GGS disaggregation (para. 14).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">The core definition (para. 15)</div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">General Government Sector (GGS)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">All organizational entities of the general government, as defined in STATISTICAL bases of financial reporting — derived from the System of National Accounts (2008 SNA).</div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Financial statements consolidate only CONTROLLED entities; statistical bases don't share that limitation. This Standard PROHIBITS presenting, as part of the GGS, any entity not consolidated in the government's own financial statements (para. 6) — it disaggregates what's already there, rather than adding new entities.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 — THE THREE STATISTICAL SECTORS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>The Three Statistical Sectors — GGS, PFC &amp; PNFC (paras. 17–22)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">General Government Sector (GGS)</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">All resident central, state, and local government units, social security funds at each level, and NONMARKET nonprofit institutions controlled by government — funded primarily via appropriation/allocation of taxes, dividends, other revenue, and borrowings. Typically includes departments, courts, public education, public health care. Does NOT include PFCs or PNFCs.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Public Financial &amp; Non-Financial Corporations sectors</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span><strong>PFC sector</strong> — government-controlled financial corporations/quasi-corporations/nonprofits primarily engaged in FINANCIAL intermediation and services for the market (e.g. government-controlled banks, including central banks)</span>
          <span><strong>PNFC sector</strong> — government-controlled entities producing GOODS or NONFINANCIAL services for the market (e.g. publicly owned utilities)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Commercial public sector entities are conceptually similar to public corporations/quasi-corporations, but the mapping isn't always exact — e.g. a NON-RESIDENT commercial public sector entity wouldn't classify as a PFC or PNFC (para. 22).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 — RELATIONSHIP TO SEGMENT REPORTING ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Relationship to Segment Reporting (paras. 7–8)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">GGS disclosure does NOT replace IPSAS 18 segments</div>
        <div class="principle">IPSAS 18 segments are based on service delivery activities and resource allocation — NOT on the market/nonmarket distinction used for GGS. GGS information alone won't let users evaluate performance against major service delivery objectives when those are achieved through NON-GGS entities.</div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">Example: identifying the GGS as a "segment" tells users nothing about a government's performance in telecommunications, healthcare, or education objectives delivered through government corporations or quasi-corporations. A government must still present IPSAS 18 segment information alongside any GGS disclosure — omitting it would leave out important information (para. 8).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 — STATISTICAL BASES VS IPSAS FINANCIAL STATEMENTS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Statistical Bases of Financial Reporting vs IPSAS Statements (paras. 9–11)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Different objectives, some shared DNA</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Framework</span><span>Primary objective</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>IPSAS financial statements</span><span>Decision-usefulness AND accountability for controlled resources</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Statistical bases (2008 SNA-derived)</span><span>Analyzing and evaluating FISCAL POLICY, especially GGS and broader public sector performance</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Both adopt an accrual basis, deal with similar transactions, and share some report structure — but the underlying accounting system may differ in important ways from the statistics ultimately derived from it (para. 9).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Why bother disclosing GGS in financial statements?</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Assists users to understand resources allocated to GGS service delivery, and financial performance in delivering those services</span>
          <span>Assists users to understand the relationship between the GGS and the corporations sectors, and each one's impact on overall financial performance</span>
          <span>Forms a useful LINK between IPSAS financial statements and statistical reports, helping users reconcile the two (para. 11)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Where government budgets are prepared for the GGS rather than the whole government, GGS disclosures under this Standard become directly relevant to IPSAS 24's budget-vs-actual comparison requirement.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 — ACCOUNTING POLICIES FOR GGS DISCLOSURES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Accounting Policies for GGS Disclosures (paras. 23–32)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Same policies as the consolidated statements — with ONE exception</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">GGS disclosures use the SAME recognition, measurement &amp; presentation policies as the consolidated financial statements</div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush"><strong>The one exception:</strong> the GGS does NOT apply IPSAS 35 consolidation to entities in the PFC/PNFC sectors — instead, it recognizes its investment in those sectors AT THE CARRYING AMOUNT OF THEIR NET ASSETS (paras. 24–25). Balances/transactions WITHIN the GGS ARE eliminated (per IPSAS 35); balances/transactions BETWEEN the GGS and other sectors are NOT eliminated (para. 30).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Why not full consolidation with PFC/PNFC?</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Statistical bases of reporting portray the GGS's impact on the whole public sector (and, per the 2008 SNA, the national economy) — presenting entities OUTSIDE the GGS as investments in other sectors, with transactions NOT eliminated. Applying full IPSAS 35 consolidation instead would simply re-present the government's ordinary consolidated statements, not a genuine GGS disaggregation (paras. 28–29).</div>
        <div class="note">Measurement can also genuinely diverge: statistical bases generally revalue all assets/liabilities (except loans) to market value each period; IPSAS permits historical cost AND current value models depending on asset class — so GGS figures in the financial statements may differ from the equivalent statistical-basis GGS figures (para. 32).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 — FURTHER DISAGGREGATION ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Further Disaggregation (paras. 33–34)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Optional deeper cuts across levels of government</div>
        <div class="principle">Where a national government's consolidated statements encompass MULTIPLE levels of government (e.g. it controls provincial/local governments), a government MAY (but is NOT required to) further disaggregate GGS information to show the GGS separately AT EACH LEVEL of government — helping users understand the relationship between each level's GGS activities and between the financial statements and statistical bases in that jurisdiction.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 — REQUIRED DISCLOSURES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Required Disclosures (paras. 35, 40)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Minimum content (para. 35)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Assets by major class — showing the investment in OTHER sectors separately</span>
          <span>Liabilities by major class</span>
          <span>Net assets/equity</span>
          <span>Total revaluation increments/decrements and other items recognized directly in net assets/equity</span>
          <span>Revenue by major class</span>
          <span>Expenses by major class</span>
          <span>Surplus or deficit</span>
          <span>Cash flows from operating, investing, and financing activities (each by major class where specified)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush"><strong>Prominence cap:</strong> the manner of GGS presentation shall be NO MORE PROMINENT than the government's own IPSAS financial statements — via note disclosure, additional columns, or another appropriate jurisdiction-specific method (paras. 35, 37).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Controlled entities included in the GGS (para. 40)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Disclose the SIGNIFICANT controlled entities included in the GGS</span>
          <span>Disclose any CHANGES in those entities from the prior period, with an explanation of why a previously-included entity is no longer included</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IPSAS 35 separately requires disclosure of significant controlled entities in the CONSOLIDATED statements as a whole — this cross-reference lets users see which of THOSE entities also sit within the GGS, and track that relationship over time (paras. 41–42). COFOG (Classification of Functions of Government) disaggregation is neither required nor prohibited (para. 38).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 — RECONCILIATIONS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Reconciliations (paras. 43–46)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">To the consolidated financial statements — mandatory</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">GGS disclosures SHALL be reconciled to the consolidated financial statements, showing the adjustment to EACH equivalent item separately</div>
        </div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Presented separately: the adjustment for the PFC/PNFC investment carrying-amount (§5), plus adjustments for each item disclosed under para. 35. Entities MAY (not required to) further split the PFC adjustment from the PNFC adjustment (para. 44).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">To statistical bases of financial reporting — NOT required</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Statistical bases and IPSAS have real differences beyond measurement — e.g. statistical bases treat DIVIDENDS as expenses, while IPSAS treats them as distributions; statistical bases distinguish transactions from "other economic flows" in ways the consolidated statements don't currently reflect.</div>
        <div class="callout success">This Standard does NOT require a reconciliation of GGS financial-statement disclosures to GGS statistical-basis disclosures — practicability, cost, and benefit concerns vary too much across jurisdictions. Including one by way of NOTE DISCLOSURE is not precluded, though (para. 46).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 — STATEMENT TEMPLATE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Statement Template — Illustrative GGS Statement of Financial Position</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Four-column format — GGS, PFC &amp; PNFC, Eliminations, Total Whole-of-Government</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;text-align:center;"><span>Line item</span><span>GGS</span><span>PFC &amp; PNFC</span><span>Eliminations</span><span>Total W-of-G</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span><strong>ASSETS — Current</strong></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span>Cash and cash equivalents, receivables, prepayments</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span style="text-align:center">(X)</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span>Inventories, investments, other current assets</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span></span><span style="text-align:center">X</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span><strong>Non-current</strong></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span>Investment in OTHER SECTORS</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span></span><span style="text-align:center">(X)</span><span style="text-align:center">—</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span>Infrastructure, plant &amp; equipment; land &amp; buildings; intangibles</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span></span><span style="text-align:center">X</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span><strong>TOTAL ASSETS</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>X</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>X</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>(X)</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>X</strong></span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span><strong>LIABILITIES — Current</strong></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span>Payables, short-term borrowings, provisions, employee benefits</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span style="text-align:center">(X)</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span><strong>Non-current</strong> — payables, borrowings, provisions, employee benefits</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span style="text-align:center">(X)</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span><strong>TOTAL LIABILITIES</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>X</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>X</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>(X)</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>X</strong></span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span><strong>NET ASSETS</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>X</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>X</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>(X)</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>X</strong></span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span>Reserves; accumulated surpluses/(deficits)</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span><span style="text-align:center">(X)</span><span style="text-align:center">X</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr;"><span><strong>TOTAL NET ASSETS/EQUITY</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>X</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>X</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>(X)</strong></span><span style="text-align:center"><strong>X</strong></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The Handbook's full Implementation Guidance carries this same four-column structure through a Statement of Financial Performance (by Classification of Function of Government), a Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity, and a Cash Flow Statement — each showing GGS, PFC and PNFC, eliminations, and total whole-of-government columns, with comparatives for two years.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 — EFFECTIVE DATE & AMENDMENT TIMELINE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Effective Date &amp; Amendment Timeline (paras. 47–48)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Amendment history at a glance</div>
        <div class="principle">Base Standard: periods beginning on/after <strong>1 January 2008</strong>.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>Effective</span><span>Change</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2017</span><span>Para. 48 (IPSAS 33); para. 13 (Improvements to IPSAS 2015)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>—</span><span>Paras. 24, 26, 27, 29, 30, 41 amended — IPSAS 35, Consolidated Financial Statements, applied alongside</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2018</span><span>Para. 16 deleted; paras. 3, 22 amended — The Applicability of IPSAS (Apr 2016)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2019</span><span>Para. 37 — Improvements to IPSAS, 2018</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2023</span><span>Paras. 5, 18, 28 — Improvements to IPSAS, 2021</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2025</span><span>Para. 32 — IPSAS 46, Measurement</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 — RELATIONSHIP TO STATISTICAL REPORTING STANDARDS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Relationship to Statistical Reporting Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">No IAS/IFRS equivalent — a genuinely public-sector-only Standard</div>
        <div class="principle">Unlike most IPSAS, IPSAS 22 is NOT drawn from any IFRS Standard — there is no private-sector "GGS" concept to converge with. Instead, it bridges IPSAS financial reporting with STATISTICAL bases of financial reporting, principally the UN's System of National Accounts (2008 SNA), and related frameworks such as the IMF's Government Finance Statistics Manual (GFSM).</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>The GGS/PFC/PNFC sector definitions in this Standard are IMPORTED directly from statistical reporting terminology, not developed independently by the IPSASB</span>
          <span>Measurement can genuinely diverge — statistical bases typically mark most assets/liabilities to market value each period; IPSAS permits historical cost for many classes</span>
          <span>Statistical bases classify dividends as EXPENSES; IPSAS classifies them as DISTRIBUTIONS — a structural difference no reconciliation requirement bridges</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Where a jurisdiction ALSO produces GFS-basis fiscal reports, GGS disclosures under this Standard are the natural "bridge" a preparer builds toward reconciling the two frameworks — even though this Standard itself doesn't mandate that reconciliation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 — STATEMENT / PROCESS MAPPING ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement / Process Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">GGS disclosure decision map</div>
        <ol class="ordered-list">
        <li>Does the government ELECT to disclose GGS information? If no &rarr; this Standard simply doesn't apply (§1)</li>
        <li>Classify each consolidated entity: GGS (nonmarket, appropriation-funded) or PFC/PNFC (market-facing, commercial)? (§2)</li>
        <li>Apply the SAME accounting policies as the consolidated statements — EXCEPT recognize the PFC/PNFC investment at the carrying amount of their net assets, not full consolidation (§5)</li>
        <li>Eliminate WITHIN-GGS balances/transactions; do NOT eliminate GGS-to-other-sector balances/transactions (§5)</li>
        <li>Present the minimum disclosure set (§7), reconciled item-by-item to the consolidated financial statements (§8)</li>
        <li>Still present IPSAS 18 segment information separately — GGS disclosure never substitutes for it (§3)</li>
      </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Concept-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>IPSAS 22 concept</span><span>Statement(s)</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>GGS assets, liabilities, net assets/equity</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Additional columns, or a separate GGS statement</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>GGS revenue, expenses, surplus/deficit</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOFPerf</span></span><span>Same structure as the primary statement</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>GGS revaluation increments/decrements</span><span><span class="badge be">NA/E</span></span><span>Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity equivalent</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>GGS cash flows</span><span><span class="badge bs">Cash Flow</span></span><span>Operating, investing, financing — mirroring IPSAS 2</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Reconciliation to consolidated totals</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory, item-by-item</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Significant controlled entities in the GGS</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>List, plus changes from the prior period</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 — RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IPSAS 22 connects across the IPSAS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 1</span><span>Defines the complete set of financial statements that GGS disclosures parallel (§7)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 2</span><span>Cash flow classification structure mirrored in GGS cash flow disclosures (§7, §9)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 3</span><span>Underlying qualitative-characteristics basis for GGS accounting policy development (§4)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 18</span><span>Segment reporting remains mandatory alongside any GGS disclosure — not a substitute (§3)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 24</span><span>Where a budget is prepared for the GGS specifically, GGS disclosures under this Standard support that comparison (§4)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 35</span><span>Consolidation basis for WITHIN-GGS eliminations; the one exception being PFC/PNFC investment treatment (§5)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 46</span><span>Measurement basis interactions affecting how GGS asset/liability figures may diverge from statistical-basis figures (§5)</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 — GLOSSARY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">General Government Sector (GGS)</div><div class="glossary-def">All organizational entities of general government, as defined under statistical bases of financial reporting.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Public Financial Corporations (PFC) sector</div><div class="glossary-def">Government-controlled entities primarily engaged in financial intermediation/services for the market.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Public Non-Financial Corporations (PNFC) sector</div><div class="glossary-def">Government-controlled entities producing goods or nonfinancial services for the market.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">2008 SNA</div><div class="glossary-def">System of National Accounts, 2008 — the UN-prepared statistical framework underpinning GGS/PFC/PNFC sector definitions.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Statistical bases of financial reporting</div><div class="glossary-def">Reporting frameworks focused on fiscal policy analysis, consistent with and derived from the 2008 SNA.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">COFOG</div><div class="glossary-def">Classification of Functions of Government — an expense-disaggregation basis used in some statistical reporting.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

